---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_HREF, NAV_HREFS } from '../data/nav';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="For non-profits - Excel Automate | Hobart"
  description="Automating the data work in fundraising organisations - CRM imports and exports, platform migrations, external partners, reporting gaps and continuity."
  current="For non-profits"
>
  <section class="prose-section" style="padding-bottom: clamp(52px, 8vw, 96px); background: var(--page);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="hero">
      <h1 class="hero-heading" style="text-wrap: pretty;"><span class="accent">Alongside</span> your CRM</h1>
      <div class="hero-rule"></div>
      <p class="hero-standfirst" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        When you work in a fundraising organisation your data never sits still for long. It's moving between your CRM
        and all your other platforms, it's going back and forth with external partners, and sometimes it needs a bulk
        update right where it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        You end up with more admin work than you can handle, or a CRM that's always lagging behind. All the extra
        overhead can hold you back from implementing the programs you need to keep driving engagement and conversion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using <span class="accent">Modern Excel</span> alongside your CRM, you can automate a lot of these problems away.
        It's fast, flexible, and gives you full control of your data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--tint);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="dataInOut">
      <h2>Getting data in and out</h2>
      <p>
        CRMs do a lot of things well, but most of them expect you to do things their way. Either they don't give you
        the flexibility to do what you need with your data, or their customisation tools are more trouble than they're
        worth.
      </p>
      <p>
        But all you really need in order to clean, combine, compare and update every kind of data is a way to get it
        out of the CRM, and a way to get it back in. An Excel automation can sit in the middle and do all of the bulk
        updates in a way that's fast, reliable, and completely in your control.
      </p>
      <p>
        Just about every CRM lets you export your data to CSV files one way or another. It doesn't matter how clunky
        or unwieldy the file is - as long as it has the data, Excel can handle the rest. A couple of clicks to run an
        automation, and you'll have a fresh file with all of the new or transformed data ready to import back in again.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--page);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="platforms">
      <h2>Moving between platforms</h2>
      <p>
        When you start out with a new CRM it probably does most of what you need. You build your processes around it,
        and it works well enough for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        Over time though, you start coming up against limitations and you have to find workarounds. Different
        platforms offer new or better functionality - maybe you want to do crowdfunding through Raisely, petitions in
        Do Gooder and email campaigns in Mailchimp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Getting data out of a system is easy, but bringing it back into a CRM that has its own unique format and
        structure can turn into a headache. Imports that should be done daily only happen weekly. Reports get out of
        date. Supporters call up with an issue and you have to look in three different places.
      </p>
      <p>
        Worse, if you're handling the data manually mistakes can be made. One file gets missed, another one gets
        imported twice. A formula points to the wrong row, or a copy paste goes awry. Making sure nothing goes wrong
        can take as long as doing the job itself. If something does go wrong, fixing it in the CRM can take a lot
        longer.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the kind of process that Power Query in Excel is built for. One click to convert the data to exactly
        the format you need, cleaned and ready for import. And you'll still have complete visibility and control of
        the data along the way, to handle any unexpected situations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--tint);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="partners">
      <h2>Working with external partners</h2>
      <p>
        As your organisation has grown it might have started exchanging data with external providers, and there might
        be more to come - a mailing house, telemarketing agency, lead sharing group, and others.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each of these exchanges usually comes with its own format requirements, and its own set of challenges. It might
        only happen a few times a year, but if finding the time to get it right puts you under pressure, automating the
        process can take a lot of that away.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--page);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="gaps">
      <h2>Filling the gaps</h2>
      <p>
        There are always going to be smaller gaps in functionality, things that your CRM isn't able to handle. Other
        CRMs might have those tools, but it's usually not practical to change CRMs every time something better comes
        along. That leaves you handling some things manually, or going without.
      </p>
      <p>
        Direct mail campaigns can be a big job when not well supported by the CRM, and mistakes can have real costs.
        Getting the segmentation and exclusions right, implementing ask ladders, removing duplicates and cleaning the
        data - all things that matter.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reporting might be lacking, with only the most basic filters and groupings and none of the nuance you need.
        Supporter analysis might end at a LYBUNT list and the rest is up to you.
      </p>
      <p>You might not need a new CRM just yet, but if there's a gap that's costing you time, an Excel automation can often fill it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose-section" style="background: var(--tint);">
    <div class="prose-col" data-reveal="continuity">
      <h2>Continuity</h2>
      <p>
        Like a lot of non-profits, your organisation might run pretty lean when it comes to staff and roles - a small
        number of people getting a lot done, with not much room for overlap. It mostly works, until the one person who
        understands that key process leaves. Turning those complex processes into robust automated tools keeps all of
        the logic in one place, and usable by anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        When building these tools in Excel there's no black box. The logic is all visible and accessible, so whoever
        picks it up next can see how it works and make changes to meet your changing needs. We're happy to keep
        supporting everything we build, but you won't be dependent on us to keep anything running.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band cta-band--green">
    <div class="cta-band-inner" data-reveal="ctaContact">
      <h2>Could your organisation use some help with its processes?</h2>
      <a href={CONTACT_HREF} class="btn btn-solid-inverse">Get in touch</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band cta-band--dark">
    <div class="cta-band-inner" data-reveal="ctaLearn">
      <h2>Why Excel and Power Query are so often the right tool for this work.</h2>
      <a href={NAV_HREFS['Learn more']} class="btn btn-ghost">Learn more</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
